comments[2]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-08-03T18:40:12.043Z","harness:codex","Verification evidence: explicit activation.commands is now authoritative across all capability combinations. Integration coverage proves a renderer/hook-capable package is not imported for unrelated list, a search+commands package activates for its own command whether or not activation.commands is declared, semantic search activates the declared search scope, and global inventory contributions still activate eagerly when required."
  "2026-08-03T19:30:43.315Z","harness:codex","Exact-head PR evidence (PR #884, c361624e2): every hosted required check passes, including static/typecheck/coverage/smokes/compat, Ubuntu/macOS/Windows runtime smoke, benchmarks, CodeQL, Semgrep, Trivy, TruffleHog, DeepScan, CodeFactor, Codecov, ShellCheck, PSScriptAnalyzer, actionlint, and Docs/Skills. The complete sandboxed local suite passes 428 files / 6,792 tests at exact 100% statements, branches, functions, and lines; lint, zero-clone analysis, 100% docstring gates, contracts, and focused review regressions pass. All 12 CodeRabbit threads were individually replied to and reacted to; nine actionable findings were fixed in c361624e2 and all threads are resolved. The requested exact-head CodeRabbit rerun was included-review-rate-limited and the authenticated Greptile gate returned free_reviews_limit_reached; these are reviewer limitations, not clean-review claims."
notes[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-08-03T18:40:12.429Z","harness:codex","Compatibility boundary: legacy packages without explicit activation.commands or static contribution data keep the conservative activation heuristic. The governance-audit package intentionally omits a narrow activation block because its parser/hooks/services are global contributions."
learnings[2]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-28T10:34:42.034Z","harness:claude-code","A gate that is opt-in via a manifest field is only as strong as the code path that reads the field. Here the field is read — and then overridden by a capability check that runs first, so the declaration is inert for exactly the extensions that most need it. The general check: when a config field is supposed to narrow behaviour, find the branch that returns the broad answer and confirm the narrow branch is evaluated before it, not after. Reading the parser alone would have shown the field being parsed correctly.\n\nThe proof that mattered was not source reading but the shipped scaffold: generate the artifact the product tells authors to generate, install it, and observe whether the runtime honours what it emitted. `pm extension --init --capability renderers` produces a manifest declaring a narrow scope, and `pm list --limit 1 --profile` shows that extension loaded anyway. One command each way, and it cannot be argued with."
  "2026-08-03T18:40:12.853Z","harness:codex",Activation metadata must be evaluated before broad capability fallback. Treating an explicit scope as authoritative makes generated manifests enforceable contracts; a static inventory then supplies the safe path for global contributions without importing unrelated modules.
